.TH OPENALLOY 1 "October 2026" "OpenAlloy 1.6.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
openalloy \- configure the lighting of the HyperX Alloy Origins Core keyboard
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B openalloy
.RI [ global-options ]
.I command
.RI [ arguments ]
.br
.B openalloy gui
.br
.B openalloy \-\-help
|
.B \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B openalloy
is a native Linux configurator for the HyperX Alloy Origins Core TKL keyboard
(USB IDs 0951:16e6 and the HP version 03f0:098f) and an alternative to HyperX
NGENUITY, which exists only for Windows.
It sets per\-key RGB colors, runs animated, key\-reactive and sound\-reactive
effects, keeps a fixed color without any program running, and, on firmware
that allows it, writes effects to the three onboard profiles of the keyboard.
.PP
The keyboard is reached through
.I /dev/hidraw
nodes, either on the vendor channel (USB interface 2) or through the standard
HID LampArray interface (USB interface 3, "Dynamic Lighting").
The right one is chosen automatically; on the HP 03f0:098f with firmware 21.16
only HID LampArray works.
.PP
When the user service
.BR openalloyd (1)
is running, every command is forwarded to it over a Unix socket and takes effect
at once.
Without the service, the command drives the keyboard by itself while it runs.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help and exit.
Every command also accepts
.BR \-\-help .
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show the version and exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-lang " CODE"
Language of this command only:
.B en
(default) or
.BR pt_BR .
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print machine\-readable JSON (for
.BR status ,
.BR effects ", and others)."
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
More detailed log on standard error.
.TP
.B \-\-simulate
Use a simulated keyboard (vendor channel, firmware 21.08).
Nothing is sent to real hardware and onboard writes are not gated.
.TP
.B \-\-simulate\-hp
Simulate the HP 03f0:098f with firmware 21.16 (vendor channel refused,
HID LampArray only).
.TP
.BR \-\-sim\-layout " " ansi | abnt2 | iso
Layout of the simulated keyboard (default
.BR abnt2 ).
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Lighting
.TP
.B status
Keyboard, driver, mode, effect and brightness.
.TP
.B effects
List the effects with their parameters, defaults and ranges.
.TP
.BI apply " EFFECT"
.RB [ \-p
.IR NAME = VALUE ]...
.RB [ \-c
.IR COLOR ]
.RB [ \-\-colors
.IR LIST ]
.RB [ \-s
.IR SPEED ]
.RB [ \-d
.IR DIRECTION ]
.RB [ \-k
.IR KEY = COLOR ]...
.RB [ \-\-keep\-keys ]
.RB [ \-r " none" | flash | ripple ]
.RB [ \-\-react\-color
.IR COLOR ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-save ]
.br
Apply an effect live.
.B \-c
and
.BR \-\-colors ,
.BR \-s " and " \-d
are shortcuts for the
.BR color ,
.BR colors ,
.BR speed " and " direction
parameters.
.B \-k
paints keys on top of the effect.
.B \-r
adds a key\-press reaction layer (light the key, or a ripple) whose color may be
.BR rainbow .
The choice is saved and restored by the service unless
.B \-\-no\-save
is given.
.TP
.BI hold " COLOR"
.RB [ \-k
.IR KEY = COLOR ]...
.RB [ \-\-preset
.IR NAME ]
.RB [ \-b
.IR LEVEL ]
.RB [ \-\-stop\-service ]
.br
Set a fixed color once and stop sending (HID LampArray keyboards): the color
stays on the keyboard with nothing running.
The firmware loses it on unplug, reboot or suspend; the service, when running,
puts it back by itself.
.TP
.B hold \-\-release
Hand the LEDs back to the keyboard firmware.
.TP
.BR brightness " [" \fILEVEL\fR ]
Show or set the brightness:
.BR 0 \- 255 ,
.IR N %,
.B +
or
.BR \- .
Hardware brightness on the vendor channel, software brightness on HID LampArray.
.TP
.BR preset " " list
.TQ
.BR preset " " apply | save | delete " " \fINAME\fR
.TQ
.BR preset " " export " " \fINAME\fR " " \fIFILE\fR.json
.TQ
.BR preset " " import " " \fIFILE\fR.json
Manage presets.
Nineteen presets are built in;
.B save
stores the current lighting under
.IR NAME .
.SS Onboard memory (vendor channel only)
.TP
.BR onboard " " read
Read the key\-press layer of the three profiles.
.TP
.BR onboard " " write " " \fIPROFILE\fR " [" \fIEFFECT\fR "] [" \fIapply-options\fR "] [" \-\-frames " " \fIN\fR "] [" \-\-keypress " " \fIMODE\fR "] [" \-y ]
Render the effect (default: the current one) into
.I N
frames (1\-255) and write it to profile 1, 2 or 3, where it runs without any
software.
.I MODE
is the key\-press layer:
.BR match ,
.BR black ,
.B keep
or a color.
Writing is locked until
.B test\-rgb
succeeds on that keyboard (see
.BR SAFETY ).
.TP
.BR profile " [" 1 | 2 | 3 ]
Show or select the active onboard profile.
.TP
.B release
Stop live mode; the keyboard shows its own (onboard) lighting.
.TP
.B resume
Return to live mode.
.TP
.BR test\-rgb " [" \-\-yes | \-\-no ]
Show a test pattern (green F\-row, red left half, blue right half) and ask
whether it appeared.
A yes unlocks onboard writes for that keyboard and firmware version.
.SS Settings and system
.TP
.BR settings " [" \-\-set " " \fIKEY\fR = \fIVALUE\fR "]..."
Show or change settings.
Values are read as JSON when possible, otherwise as text.
See
.BR SETTINGS .
.TP
.BR language " [" \fICODE\fR ]
Show or save the interface language
.RB ( en ", " pt_BR ).
The service and an open window switch too.
.TP
.B gui
Open the graphical interface (GTK 4).
.TP
.B daemon
Run the service in the foreground (same as
.BR openalloyd ).
.TP
.B doctor
Diagnose the installation: interfaces found, their roles and permissions, udev
rule, service.
Nothing is sent to the keyboard.
.TP
.B udev\-rules
Print the udev rule that grants the logged\-in user access to the lighting
interfaces (2 and 3) only.
.TP
.BR service " " start | stop | restart | status | enable | disable
Control the systemd user service.
.SH KEYS AND COLORS
A
.I KEY
is a name
.RB ( w ", " space ", " leftshift ", " enter ),
a LED index
.RB ( 19 )
or a group:
.BR wasd ,
.BR arrows ,
.BR letters ,
.BR numbers ,
.BR frow ,
.BR modifiers ,
.BR nav .
A
.I COLOR
is
.BI # rrggbb
or a name such as
.BR red ,
.BR white ,
.BR blue .
.SH EFFECTS
.BR static ,
.BR breathing ,
.BR spectrum ,
.BR wave ,
.BR colorwave ,
.BR gradient ,
.BR scanner ,
.BR fire ,
.BR snake ,
.BR starlight ,
.BR rain ,
.BR flicker ,
.BR blink ,
.BR off ;
key\-reactive (live only):
.BR reactive ,
.BR ripple ;
sound\-reactive (live only, system audio through
.BR parec (1)
or
.BR pw\-record (1)):
.BR equalizer ,
.BR soundspectrum ,
.BR soundpulse ,
.BR soundwave .
Run
.B openalloy effects
for their parameters.
.SH SETTINGS
.TP
.BR layout_override " (" null ", " ansi ", " abnt2 ", " iso )
Force the drawn layout; null detects it from the keyboard.
.TP
.BR led_gamma " (1.0\-3.0, default 2.2)"
Convert screen colors to LED colors; 1.0 sends them unchanged.
.TP
.BR fps " (5\-60, default 30)"
Frame rate of live effects.
.TP
.BR profile_key_action " (" release ", " bind ", " ignore )
What Fn+1/2/3 does: show the onboard profile, or apply the preset bound in
.BR profile_bindings .
.TP
.BR reactive_input " (true, false)"
Read key presses for the reactive effects.
.TP
.B audio_source
PulseAudio/PipeWire source for sound effects; empty means the monitor of the
default output.
.TP
.BR onboard_frames ", " onboard_keypress ", " onboard_keypress_color
Defaults for
.BR "onboard write" .
.TP
.B language
Interface language.
.SH SAFETY
Onboard writes go to the keyboard's flash memory, which has no public
documentation.
They start locked on every real keyboard and are unlocked per keyboard (USB ID
and serial number) and firmware version by a successful
.BR test\-rgb .
The 780\-byte profile storage is read and written back unchanged (the write is
refused if it cannot be read), and command 0x30 without parameters, which
resets the firmware, is never sent.
Arbitrary HID commands cannot be sent.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B OPENALLOY_LANG
Interface language when no
.B \-\-lang
is given.
.TP
.B OPENALLOY_SOCKET
Path of the service socket.
.TP
.B OPENALLOY_ENABLE_FLASH
Set to
.B I_ACCEPT_THE_RISK
in the service environment to unlock onboard writes without
.BR test\-rgb .
.TP
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME ", " XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Locations of the settings and of the socket.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/openalloy/config.json
Settings.
.TP
.I ~/.config/openalloy/presets/
User presets (JSON).
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/openalloy.sock
Service socket.
.TP
.I /etc/udev/rules.d/60\-openalloy.rules
Access to the lighting interfaces (2 and 3) for the local user.
.TP
.I /etc/udev/rules.d/61\-openalloy\-reactive.rules
Optional: read access to this keyboard's key events (reactive effects).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B 0
on success,
.B 1
on error,
.B 2
on a usage error,
.B 130
when interrupted.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
openalloy apply wave \-s 40 \-d left
openalloy apply static \-c \(dq#e31937\(dq \-k wasd=white \-k arrows=#00b7ff
openalloy apply colorwave \-r ripple \-\-react\-color rainbow
openalloy apply equalizer \-p sensitivity=150
openalloy hold red \-k wasd=white
openalloy brightness 50%
openalloy preset apply matrix
openalloy settings \-\-set layout_override=ansi
openalloy \-\-simulate\-hp \-\-sim\-layout ansi gui
.fi
.SH BUGS
Key macros and hardware key remapping are not supported (the protocol is
unknown for this keyboard); use
.BR keyd (1)
or input\-remapper.
Do not run OpenRGB or another lighting tool on the same keyboard at the same
time.
